  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004911",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches to the target trait (hypertrophic cardiomyopathy) via trait_reported/trait_efo. Among them, PGS004911 reports the strongest visible predictive performance (AUC=0.8, R2=0.048) with a large evaluation sample size (n=343,182) and a modern genome-wide method (PRS-CS), all within the same 2025 Nat Genet publication context as PGS004910. PGS000739 shows a higher AUC (0.821) but uses far fewer variants (27) and is older; without additional context on evaluation design comparability across studies, the AUC values are not directly comparable, lowering certainty. PGS000778 is less suitable as a general HCM PRS because the reported phenotype is a quantitative wall-thickness measure in sarcomere-variant carriers and has a very small validation sample (n=368) with no performance metrics. Confidence is Moderate due to unresolved cross-study comparability between PGS004911 and PGS000739 despite both being direct-match models with reported AUC."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005218",
  "confidence": "High",
  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match PGS are available for hypothyroidism (28 total). PGS005218 is the most strongly supported in the provided context because it is explicitly for hypothyroidism, has the largest reported training sample size (n=1,146,562) with a large validation sample (n=441,692), and it reports the highest AUC among the listed direct-match models (AUC=0.859). Other candidates either target narrower/atypical phenotypes (e.g., anti-PD-(L)1 induced hypothyroidism, time-to-event or ICD subcode E03), have substantially lower AUCs when reported (e.g., ~0.64–0.77), or lack performance metrics in the context. Main limitation: the context does not provide calibration or additional metrics beyond AUC for most models, but within the available evidence PGS005218 is clearly best supported."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005218",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All listed candidates are direct matches to the target trait (hypothyroidism) at the EFO level, but they vary in phenotype definition and reported validation. PGS005218 is best supported in this context because it is explicitly a hypothyroidism PRS from a dedicated large GWAS (training n=1,146,562) with a large validation sample (n=441,692) and it reports the strongest discrimination among the models that provide AUC (AUC=0.859). Key limitation: most other direct-match models do not report AUC/R2 here, and some alternatives target different hypothyroidism definitions (e.g., time-to-event, ICD E03 subtype, immunotherapy-induced hypothyroidism), so while PGS005218 is the clearest choice from available evidence, comparability across phenotypes and missing performance metrics for many candidates prevent high confidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004908",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 10 returned models are direct matches to kidney cancer/renal carcinoma/renal cell carcinoma. Among candidates with reported discrimination, PGS004908 has the highest AUC (0.74) in a large EUR evaluation sample (n=324,805) and is based on a recent, kidney-cancer-specific multi-ancestry GWAS (107 variants). Competing direct-match models either have lower AUC (e.g., PGS000076/PGS000787 AUC=0.722; PGS000722 AUC=0.567; PGS000605/000606 AUC=0.517; PGS004690 AUC=0.5644) or lack reported AUC. Confidence is not High because key comparability details are missing/unequal across models (many have unknown covariates and/or no metrics; phenotyping differs across 'kidney cancer' vs 'malignant neoplasm of kidney, except pelvis' vs 'renal cell carcinoma'), so the AUC values may not be strictly head-to-head."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004944",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All five candidates are direct matches to open-angle glaucoma (reported as primary open-angle glaucoma; EFO: open-angle glaucoma). Among models with reported discrimination, PGS004944 (lassosum; 144,019 variants) has AUC 0.748 with a very large validation sample size (n=407,667), comparable AUC to PGS001797 (AUC 0.749; n=7,128) and higher than PGS002741 (AUC 0.69; n=3,382); PGS000350 and PGS000764 report no AUC. The main limitation is that PGS004944’s phenotyping is explicitly noted as self-reported in the context and evaluation ancestry is MAE (100%), so despite strong sample size support, applicability and phenotype fidelity relative to clinically confirmed POAG remain somewhat uncertain from the provided evidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001843",
  "confidence": "Low",
  "rationale": "All four retrieved models are plausibly related to the target, but only two (PGS001843, PGS002055) are an exact direct-match to the target trait via trait_efo=\"peripheral vascular disease\" and trait_reported \"Peripheral vascular disease, unspecified\". Neither of these provides visible performance metrics (AUC/R2 are null), and they come from the same paper/cohort context, making it difficult to clearly prefer one on predictive performance. PGS005217 and PGS005158 are for \"Peripheral artery disease\" (different EFO term than the query) and therefore are not direct matches to the stated target trait. Between the two direct matches, PGS001843 is selected only because it has a clearer, more parsimonious model size (242 variants) with the same reported evaluation ancestries and validation sample size as PGS002055, but the lack of any reported performance metrics means the recommendation remains weak."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001536",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are EFO-matched to vitiligo, but they differ in how directly their phenotyping aligns with vitiligo and in available performance evidence. PGS001536 is explicitly developed/validated for vitiligo (time-to-event vitiligo) with reported discrimination (AUC 0.82774) and variance explained (R2 0.08055) and a large evaluation sample (n=67,425) with multi-ancestry evaluation, making it the best-supported option in the provided context. Limitations remain: its endpoint is time-to-event vitiligo (not necessarily directly comparable to case/control vitiligo), and the other vitiligo-labeled scores have either weaker reported R2 with smaller evaluation (PGS000738; R2 0.0325; n=4,987) or have phenotyping that appears mismatched despite the vitiligo label (PGS000760 phenotyping reported as anti-PD-L1 induced hypothyroidism), which reduces certainty that any single model is unequivocally optimal for general vitiligo."
}
